 :implied-cost
 {:model-as-the-receipt-wrote-it
  {:hops 4 :plus-prolly-tree-waves 2 :round-trips 6
   :handler-cost-ms [3974 4437]
   :implied-per-round-trip-ms [662.3 739.5]}
  :this-does-not-reconcile
  "ADR-2607310900 measured roughly 90-250ms per round trip, and the 08-17 receipt
   cited its own 173-190ms as sitting comfortably inside that range. 662-740ms is 3
   to 8 times outside it. The receipt's arithmetic was self-consistent only because
   two errors pointed opposite ways: it over-counted hops (21 vs 4) and therefore
   under-counted the cost of each. Correcting the hop count does not repair the
   model, it breaks it."
  :what-this-means
  "The novelty NODE walk is NOT the dominant term in the 4.0-7.1s handler cost. Four
   sequential fetches cannot account for four seconds at any per-hop cost this
   codebase has ever measured."

  :where-the-time-more-plausibly-goes
  {:status :hypothesis-from-source-reading-not-measured
   :read-every-entry
   "Hydration does not stop at enumerating novelty. hydrate-chain-cached calls
    read-tx-block for EVERY novelty entry, not every node -- 63 encrypted tx blocks,
    each fetched and AEAD-decrypted. Those go through pmap-async, whose
    pmap-async-batch-size is 24, so they cost ceil(63/24) = 3 more sequential waves
    plus 63 decryptions. Segmentation compressed the NODE walk from 63 to 4; it does
    nothing to this per-ENTRY term, which still scales with novelty_size."
   :snapshot-scan
   "The other half is hydrate-db-cached over the indexed snapshot, which scans a
    whole prolly-tree index. The current snapshot's spo root is an INTERNAL node with
    20 children, so a full scan touches at least 21 blocks -- already more than the 2
    'waves' the receipt's model allowed for the entire tree. The full tree was not
    measured, so no total is claimed here."
   :not-claimed "This receipt does not claim to have explained the 4 seconds. It
                 establishes only that the novelty node walk is not what is spending
                 them, and names two candidates that were mis-sized in the model."}

  :prediction-now-in-doubt
  "The 08-17 receipt's :check-when-it-fires makes an explicit falsifiable prediction:
   'with novelty at ~0 the sequential term is the prolly-tree's 2 waves, so handler
   cost should fall from ~4.4s to well under 1s. If it does not, the novelty chain
   was not the dominant term after all and this receipt's arithmetic is wrong.'
   That test is still worth running, but it should be run expecting it to FAIL, for
   two reasons this walk supplies. First, the novelty term being removed is 4 node
   hops plus 3 tx-block waves, not 21 hops. Second, a fold does not only remove
   novelty -- it writes a LARGER snapshot (seq 909 replaced the snapshot CID), and
   the snapshot scan is the half that novelty_size does not bound. Folding may move
   cost rather than remove it."}

